# Scan 1 — Thesis and narrative focus

## What works
# Scan 2 — Structure and pacing

## Current effective structure

The current body has roughly these movements:

1. edition note;
2. incident;
3. origin story;
4. series/thesis callout;
5. market validation;
6. four assurances;
7. artifact definition/taxonomy;
8. long reviewer lifecycle;
9. control fabric;
10. outcome evidence;
11. four non-equivalences;
12. where system lied;
13. lab implementation status;
14. receipt rail;
15. evidence-label legend;
16. estate architecture figures;
17. reading path;
18. close.

This is too many structural movements for a 14-minute essay.

## Recommended six-movement structure

### 1. The fix existed. The projects were still wrong.
- cold open;
- stale reviewer;
- first figure;
- thesis question.

### 2. The harder problem: not every difference should disappear.
- managed variation;
- three-project example;
- signature diagram.

### 3. What the registry wave actually solved.
- concise market evidence;
- artifact definition;
- why package semantics matter;
- one compact prior-art note.

### 4. From artifact to use.
- source/binding/deployment/use;
- one shared thread visual instead of six repetitive figures;
- four assurance boundaries emerge naturally.

### 5. The handoffs are the architecture.
- control fabric;
- cross-system identity/evidence;
- outcome boundary;
- four non-equivalences.

### 6. What I can prove today.
- receipts;
- implemented/proposed;
- one lab deep-dive link/expandable;
- close;
- reading-path handoff.

## Move "Where the system lied to us"

This is one of the best section titles in the draft.

Move it **forward**, either as movement 2 or as a recurring motif.

Possible pattern:

> The system lied in four increasingly important ways:
>
> - "fixed" did not mean deployed;
> - same name did not mean same artifact;
> - present did not mean followed;
> - traceable did not mean successful.

That gives the abstract assurances a memorable narrative form.

