`WhereThisSits` already performs the cross-essay navigation role.

If desired, move a one-line thesis context into the right rail or the reading-path component.

### Keep and upgrade "Four things that are not equivalent"

This is worth a bespoke `BoundaryGrid`:

| Authenticity | ≠ Safety |
| Authorization | ≠ Correctness |
| Traceability | ≠ Causality |
| Pinned inputs | ≠ Determinism |

Make it visually memorable.

### Convert "What this looks like in my lab" into `ImplementationStatus`

Use rows with status chips:

- IMPLEMENTED
- PARTIAL / BOUNDED
- PROPOSED
- TO TEST

The current prose is useful but easier to scan as status data.

### Move Evidence Labels into a legend

Do not give it a full blue callout late in the essay.

Use:
- a small legend near the first `ClaimBadge`;
- a hover help icon;
- or a footer disclosure.

---

## Pull quotes

Use no more than 3–4 displayed pull quotes.

### Tier 1

> **Copy and paste is fine for discovery, but it will never be a lifecycle.**

Best placement:
after the origin story.

### Tier 1

> **For five days, "fixed" described one location.**

Best placement:
midway through the managed-variation/deployment section.

### Tier 1

> **A configured integration is not enforcement; an advisory registration is not a gate.**

Best placement:
control-fabric section.

### Tier 1, thesis mark

> **Trusted artifact ≠ trusted use.**

This should be styled as a thesis marker rather than a conventional blockquote.

### Optional

> **A skill is small enough to install and large enough to carry intent.**

Use only if the taxonomy section remains prose-heavy.

### New line worth earning

After the managed-variation section:


- [ ] Clean ThreadRail nested-scroll behavior.
- [ ] Simplify/fix interactive ARIA semantics.
- [ ] Add pull quotes, max 3–4.
- [ ] Add mobile-specific technical-figure treatments.
- [ ] Consider new title/deck after final narrative pass.

---

# Pull-quote shortlist

## Use

1. **"Copy and paste is fine for discovery, but it will never be a lifecycle."**
2. **"For five days, 'fixed' described one location."**
3. **"A configured integration is not enforcement; an advisory registration is not a gate."**
4. **"Trusted artifact ≠ trusted use."** — style as thesis marker rather than ordinary quote.

## Optional

5. **"A skill is small enough to install and large enough to carry intent."**
6. **"The reviewer didn't need a better name or another catalog."**

## Suggested new line if the managed-variation section earns it

> **The goal is not one approved copy everywhere. It is knowing which differences are stale, which are deliberate, and which should become shared improvements.**

---

# Final assessment

The draft is **closer to publication intellectually than it is editorially**.

The hard thinking is mostly present:
- evidence boundaries;
- trusted artifact vs trusted use;
- cross-system handoffs;
- implementation receipts;
- source/run/outcome distinctions.

What it needs now is subtraction and hierarchy.

The final version should feel like:

> one compelling engineering failure  
> → one harder variation problem  
> → one architecture insight  
> → one bounded implementation record  
> → one clear next question.

Not:

> every useful representation of the system rendered sequentially on one page.

The Astro implementation gives enough freedom to make the page unusually good. Use that freedom to **remove cognitive load**, not to add more surfaces.
