     2	  note="First published July 2026; re-published September 18, 2026. This full September rewrite follows two external reviews and a failure in my own deployment process. The July argument centered on registries becoming supply chains. This edition sharpens the boundary: approving an artifact doesn't establish that a particular use is trustworthy. The distinction below is the September argument, not a claim about what the July essay already said."
     3	A reviewer skill used across my projects was missing a mandatory guard, so I fixed it at the source: the `/.claude/skills/` directory in my most active project. My personal registry showed the fixed version. Inside the source repository, the reviewer (obviously) had the new instruction, but every other project on the machine loaded a deployed file copy, and that copy sat five days without the fix.[^drift]
     4	The registry was right. It was my usage that was wrong. Nothing said so, because nothing compared the deployed bytes to the registered ones.
     5	**Trusted artifact ≠ trusted use.** Approval attaches to an artifact; use happens in a context the approval never saw. Even after I correct the copy (`task-completion-validator`, the reviewer skill from the opening), I still need to know who may use it, with which tools and context, what actually ran, and whether the review met its purpose.
     6	**Prior question:** How does work survive the session?  
     7	**This thesis:** artifact approval and trusted use require different evidence.  
     8	**Built and tested in:** my personal lab, using SkillMeat and local deployment checks.  
     9	**Related external work:** the registry and catalog sources below.  
    10	The remedy is a recorded, registry-driven deployment. No manual copy/paste, no symlink as a shortcut around the record. A symlink follows the source checkout as branches change, so it drifts with the branch instead of leaving stale bytes behind. Multiple git worktrees make this worse, as each checkout silently maintains its own artifact state. Either way, “it's the same skill” conceals the state that matters.
    11	Preserving Project B's patch keeps the evidence available, but that doesn't approve the patch for everyone. A useful local change can become a candidate for shared policy; it shouldn't automatically become shared policy simply because a sync command happened to encounter it. Project C's pin can travel the other way and become ineligible under a changed requirement. Both are policy questions in this example, not lab incidents I have recorded. What I want from the supply chain is that a shared correction reaches every destination that should have it, and stops at the one that shouldn't.
